# Dev-only paths kept in this repo for the maintainer's own history/tooling but
# excluded from the distributed archive. GitHub's tarball download and `git archive`
# honor export-ignore, so these never ship to a consuming repo.
#
# Without this, `do-work update` (which fetches the main-branch tarball, or falls back to
# `git archive` of a shallow clone, then untars it) would extract the maintainer's own
# queue/archive/KB and sample reports into a consumer's skill directory. They land in the
# wrong place (nested under the skill dir, not the consumer's project root) so they don't
# corrupt anything, but they're shipped clutter. Both fetch routes honor export-ignore;
# a worktree copy would not, which is why the git fallback repacks with `git archive`.
#
# /do-work and /kb are LOAD-BEARING, not housekeeping. This repo tracks both (they are the
# same Trail of Intent the skill tells consumers to commit, and the tracked-path-only guards
# — record-commit-hash.sh's size-floor/numstat checks, cleanup Pass 6 blanked-REQ recovery —
# only work on tracked REQs). These two lines are therefore the ONLY thing keeping the
# maintainer's queue and knowledge base out of consumer installs.
# _dev/tests/contract-regressions.sh asserts both lines exist; do not remove them. The dev
# dotfiles (.gitignore/.gitattributes) are export-ignore-only too, and a stale vendored copy
# leaks nothing (export-ignore is inert once the skill is a subdirectory of a consumer's repo).
#
# decisions/ holds the maintainer's own ADRs about this skill — design history, never
# loaded at runtime by any action — so consumers don't need it. The dev dotfiles
# (.gitignore/.gitattributes) are repo-maintenance plumbing that's meaningless once the
# skill is a subdirectory of someone else's repo. All are excluded for a clean install.
#
# /CHANGELOG-20*.md is the dated changelog archives (CHANGELOG-<date>-up-to-v<version>.md,
# one per release range). They are tracked so every entry ever written stays on disk and
# greppable, but they are ~60k words of history that no action reads at runtime — version.md
# only ever parses the newest few entries out of the live CHANGELOG.md, which is NOT matched
# by this glob (it requires "-20" after CHANGELOG). A new archive is covered automatically,
# so cutting one is a rename plus a pointer edit, never a .gitattributes edit that gets
# forgotten. Tarball installs read the archives on GitHub; the live CHANGELOG.md ships.
#
# CLAUDE.md/AGENTS.md are the maintainer doc for THIS repo, not runtime skill content.
# Shipped nested inside a consumer's repo, Claude Code auto-loads CLAUDE.md into context
# on every read of a skill file — a ~2.5k-word tax whose commit protocol (bump
# actions/version.md, add a CHANGELOG entry) is actively wrong advice downstream. They
# get no tar --exclude fallback, and nothing else here does either: tar --exclude matches
# basenames at any depth (the same trap as `diff -x`), and every fetch route — tarball or
# `git archive` — already honors export-ignore. Stale copies in existing installs
# are deleted by the update flow (actions/version.md Step 5) instead. Shipped files
# must never cite these two files — see _dev/primes/prime-action-files.md → Cross-Referencing.
/do-work         export-ignore
/kb              export-ignore
/CHANGELOG-20*.md export-ignore
/ai-reports      export-ignore
/.vscode         export-ignore
/_dev            export-ignore
/decisions       export-ignore
/.gitignore      export-ignore
/.gitattributes  export-ignore
/CLAUDE.md       export-ignore
/AGENTS.md       export-ignore

# The heavy-lane contract tests read the maintainer's own /_dev tree six levels up
# (the lane manifest, its runtime fingerprint helper, the maintainer gate script). In an
# installed copy that walk lands on <project>/.claude/, where /_dev has never shipped, so
# every one of them fails and takes the installed module's `go test ./...` with it. They
# cannot be made self-contained: what they pin is the REAL lane manifest, and a vendored
# fixture would assert nothing. So the one file holding all of them stays maintainer-side.
# Keep every maintainer-tree read in that file; a new one elsewhere breaks consumers again.
/skills/do-work/tools/do-work-cli/internal/heavyverification/heavy_maintainer_tree_test.go export-ignore
